  That decision makes the next one load-bearing. Records now [1mpersist[0m on the user's machine and are meant to be [1mpasted [0m
  [1minto a bug report[0m — so anything the logger touches can end up in a ticket, a Slack thread, or an email. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m today
  calls [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username)[39m and has the password in scope two lines away. The redaction policy is what
  stops a credential from riding along.

  - [1mAllowlist[0m — the logger serializes only fields a call site explicitly declares safe; anything else becomes
    [38;5;153m[redacted][39m. Buys: a new field added later is private by default, so the failure mode is a missing log line rather
    than a leaked password. Costs: every call site names its fields, and you occasionally re-deploy to see something you
    forgot to include. Hard to loosen later in the sense that call sites encode the policy — but that's the direction
    you want it stuck in.
  - [1mKey-name denylist[0m — drop keys matching [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, etc. Buys: call sites stay casual; pass an
    object, the logger cleans it. Costs: fails [3mopen[0m — [38;5;153mpwd[39m, [38;5;153mpassphrase[39m, or a whole form object serialized wholesale sails
    through. One missed name is a leak.
  - [1mHash/truncate identifiers[0m — layered on either of the above: log [38;5;153musr_9f3a…[39m instead of the username, so you can
    correlate a user's events across records without the logs naming them.

  My recommendation: [1mallowlist, with identifiers truncated[0m. There are only about four call sites in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, so the cost
  of the strict option is close to zero here, and the artifact is something users hand to you — that's exactly where
  fail-open is the wrong default. The password should never be passed to the logger at any level.
